  "facts": [
    "The Outerbridge Crossing, named after Port Authority pioneer Eugenius Harvey Outerbridge, opened June 29, 1928, and was designed by John Alexander Low Waddell as a steel cantilever bridge spanning the Arthur Kill.",
    "After the 1970 toll reform, the bridge became eastbound‑only, doubling tolls for New Jersey‑to‑New York traffic and eliminating westbound charges, a change mirrored across eleven other crossings in the region.",
    "In 2019, the Outerbridge Crossing transitioned to open‑road tolling, removing booths and installing overhead gantries that capture license plates for non‑E‑ZPass vehicles while enabling high‑speed lanes for transponder users."
  ],
